  "generated_text": "First, we note that the sum of a geometric series $1 + 7 + 7^2 + \\cdots + 7^{2004}$ can be calculated using the formula for the sum of a geometric series:\n\n\\[\nS = \\frac{1 - 7^{2005}}{1 - 7} = \\frac{1 - 7^{2005}}{-6}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to find the remainder when $S$ is divided by $1000$. We can do this by considering the properties of $7^{2005} \\mod 1000$.\n\nSince $7^{2005}$ is a large number, we can use modular arithmetic properties to simplify the calculation. Notice that $7^{2005} \\equiv 1 \\pmod{1000}$. Therefore:\n\n\\[\nS \\equiv \\frac{1 - 1}{-6} \\equiv \\frac{1000}{-6} \\pmod{1000}\n\\]\n\nThus, the remainder $N$ is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{167}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[167, '167']",
    "gold_answer": "By the geometric series formula, $1 + 7 + 7^2 + \\cdots + 7^{2004} = \\frac{7^{2005}-1}{7-1} = \\frac{7^{2005}-1}{6}$. Since $\\varphi(1000) = 400$, by Fermat-Euler's Theorem, this is equivalent to finding $\\frac{7^{400 \\cdot 5 + 5} - 1}{6} \\equiv \\frac{7^5 - 1}{6} \\equiv \\boxed{801} \\pmod{1000}$.",
    "is_correct": false
